WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:07.509
In this video, we'll look at how we can rebuild the logic controlling our dancing characters animations.

2
00:00:07.509 --> 00:00:15.210
So here we see a basic Unity animation state machine, linking two dances to get or two animations together

3
00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:19.219
with the Boolean controlling the transition between them.

4
00:00:19.219 --> 00:00:24.289
Again, this is showing the Boolean state for one of the transitions.

5
00:00:24.289 --> 00:00:30.899
Now inside of Unreal, we're going to be creating something that looks very similar to this.

6
00:00:30.899 --> 00:00:39.929
So in order to create our animation blueprint, we can right click on our skeletal mesh of our dancer,

7
00:00:39.929 --> 00:00:44.429
come up to create and go to anim blueprint.

8
00:00:44.429 --> 00:00:54.479
And I'm going to call this ABP session demo dancer.

9
00:00:54.479 --> 00:01:01.979
An important thing to note is that animation blueprints can only be tied to a specific skeleton.

10
00:01:01.979 --> 00:01:07.480
So if you report characters or bring in a lot of other characters that have different skeletons,

11
00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:10.980
you'll need different animation blueprints for each one.

12
00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:20.140
When we open it up, we see something that looks very similar to our regular animation or our regular blueprint graph.

13
00:01:20.209 --> 00:01:24.209
Except this is our animation state machine graph.

14
00:01:24.209 --> 00:01:30.709
Over here to the left, we see our regular event graph, like on a basic blueprint.

15
00:01:30.709 --> 00:01:43.140
But what we need here in our animation graph is to provide a node that will output a pose to our output pose node,

16
00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:48.140
and ultimately drive the bones of our skeleton.

17
00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:58.760
So if we right click in here, search for state machine, we can create one and we'll just leave it called new for now.

18
00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:03.900
So if we click in to the state machine of our animation graph,

19
00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:10.900
now we're going to start to see something that looks very similar to our animator in Unity.

20
00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:16.900
We can look in this out asset browser window, which will show us all the different animations in our project.

21
00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:20.900
See, we have a chicken dance that we can drag in.

22
00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:24.930
And we have a hip hop dance that we can drag in.

23
00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:29.469
So I'm actually going to orient these this way.

24
00:02:29.969 --> 00:02:38.969
So just like we would in Unity, we'll drag a transition from the entry state into our first dancer animation state.

25
00:02:38.969 --> 00:02:48.770
Then we can drag a transition from the first animation to the second and one from the second animation back to the first.

26
00:02:48.770 --> 00:03:00.479
However, now we need to set up our transitions, as well as set looping settings inside of our animations.

27
00:03:00.479 --> 00:03:07.050
If we come in here, here, we can click on the node that represents the animation file itself.

28
00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:15.050
And if we scroll down, we should see a checkbox here for a loop animation, which we want to set to true.

29
00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:23.050
We'll do we can click these on these labels up here to go up and down or to go back up in our graph.

30
00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:30.550
So we can do that and come over here to the chicken dance animation, scroll down and set this to be looping as well.

31
00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:36.759
Go ahead and compile this. And now we'll see our animation playing.

32
00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:42.810
Next, though, we need to set our transitions.

33
00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:57.569
And so if we come here and click into it, we need to give ourselves a Boolean that will control our that will control our transition.

34
00:03:57.569 --> 00:04:11.340
So over here, we can do this in the new trick we can do to create our Boolean variable is to right click on the Boolean pin of our result and click promote to variable.

35
00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:14.340
I'm going to call this chicken dance.

36
00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:29.720
So when chicken dance is true, we will transition from the hip hop dance down to our chicken dance.

37
00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:40.589
Now, if we enter into the logic for the transition that goes from our chicken dance back to our hip hop dance, we can drag.

38
00:04:40.589 --> 00:04:48.810
So we can drag in our variable from the list down here on the left, get our chicken dance.

39
00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:51.810
And then we want to get when it's not true.

40
00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:58.100
So if we type in not Boolean, you'll get this handy little conversion node.

41
00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:05.100
So whenever chicken dance is false, now we will go from the chicken dance back to the hip hop dance.

42
00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:10.230
We'll hit compile. We'll see we get no more warnings. We're all happy here.

43
00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:17.000
And our logic is fully set up on that front.

44
00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:35.279
The next thing, though, we need is a way for an outside actors to be like our player to be able to call an event on this animation blueprint that will change the value of the chicken dance Boolean.

45
00:05:35.279 --> 00:05:40.889
So once again, we can create a custom event like we did on our cube.

46
00:05:40.889 --> 00:05:46.079
We can call this session dance toggle.

47
00:05:46.079 --> 00:05:55.920
So we create a custom event called session dance toggle, and we're going to use this to set the value of our chicken dance Boolean.

48
00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:05.920
So one way we can do that is by dragging off a new reference to our chicken dance Boolean and looking for the branch node.

49
00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:12.920
This is a handy node inside of blueprints that is very similar to an if statement inside of C sharp.

50
00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:24.170
So in thinking about this and C sharp terms, if chicken dance is true, then we want to set it to false.

51
00:06:24.170 --> 00:06:32.300
If chicken dance is false and duplicate this with control D, then we want to set chicken dance to true.

52
00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:42.879
Now, this will work well and good, but there's actually another handy node that we can use inside of unreal that we get for free called flip flop.

53
00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:49.000
And so a flip flop will do the exact same thing, but without needing to do this comparison.

54
00:06:49.000 --> 00:07:03.449
It will just automatically every time we go to execute this little bit of code, the first time it will go to A and the second time it will go to B.

55
00:07:03.449 --> 00:07:10.449
Now, another neat thing we can use with this flip flop node is instead of having two different set nodes here,

56
00:07:10.449 --> 00:07:21.120
we can instead just use the one, have A and B both go into it, but use the value of A to set our chicken dance.

57
00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:32.560
So if A is true, chicken dance will be true. If B executes, A will be false and chicken dance will be false.

58
00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:39.560
This will then drive the logic inside of our state machine, switching between hip hop dance and chicken dance.

59
00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:51.029
So now if we want to see our dancer dancing in the world, we can drag her in, press play, and there she goes.

60
00:07:51.029 --> 00:07:57.029
However, we still don't have a way to call that dance toggle event yet, which we'll get to next.

61
00:07:57.029 --> 00:08:04.439
We also see that the animations are playing to the side here.

62
00:08:04.439 --> 00:08:17.160
So in order to fix that, we can open up our animation, go to reimport with dialogue, give ourselves a 90 degree offset, save.

63
00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:30.629
And let's do that for our other dance as well. Reimport with dialogue, 90 degree import, save.

64
00:08:30.629 --> 00:08:36.759
And now hopefully we will see her facing the correct way. Beautiful.

65
00:08:36.759 --> 00:08:51.919
We can also delete our statuesque.

66
00:08:51.919 --> 00:08:57.919
In the next video, we'll look at rebuilding our Unity shader graph with an Unreal Engine material.
